clueweb09-en0000-97-06228.0 Rocky Mountains - Profile of the Rocky Mountains Search [About.com] Animals / Wildlife Home Education Animals / Wildlife Email Animals / Wildlife Animal Facts Habitat Facts Watching Wildlife Free Animals / Wildlife Newsletter!Sign Up Discuss in my Forum Rocky Mountains By Laura Klappenbach, About.com See More About: habitat mountains rocky mountains []Teton Range reflection on Beaver Pond. Photo © Aimintang / iStockphooto. The Rocky Mountains stretch through western North America from Alaska to New Mexico, forming the middle section of the Western Cordillera, an extensive belt of mountains that reaches from the Arctic Circle to Mexico. The Rocky Mountains straddle the continental divide, a natural barrier to the
clueweb09-en0000-97-06228.75 Rocky Mountains - Profile of the Rocky Mountains an extensive belt of mountains that reaches from the Arctic Circle to Mexico. The Rocky Mountains straddle the continental divide, a natural barrier to the west of which water flows toward the Pacific Ocean and to the east of which water flows in the direction of the Arctic and Atlantic Oceans.Habitat Classification: The habitat zones found within the Rocky Mountains may be classified as follows: Ecozone: Terrestrial Ecosystem: Alpine / Montane Region: Nearctic Primary Habitat: Temperate forest Secondary Habitats: Aspen forest, ponderosa pine forest, high elevation willow forest, spruce / fir forest, alpine tundra Wildlife: Birds that can be found
clueweb09-enwp02-29-17635.0 Rimrock - Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia Rimrock From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia Jump to: navigation, search [] [] Rimrock in Colorado National Monument. Rimrock is the sheer rock wall at the upper edge of a plateau, canyon, or geological uplift. It may refer to either the rock formation or to the rock itself. Rimrock may be composed of almost any stoneýýýbasalt, gneiss, granite, sandstone, etc.ýýýand is frequently layered. Many times it overlays a softer stone which erodes away underneath. American Indians in the West often used cavities found below rimrock to construct dwellings and granaries. [edit] Geographic placenames [] [] Cliff Palace in Mesa Verde National
